WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.824
Perplexity charts can

00:00:01.635 --> 00:00:02.625
just reflect more

00:00:02.437 --> 00:00:02.979
code

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:04.960
Responding to OpenAI's

00:00:04.772 --> 00:00:06.021
perplexity-over-time

00:00:05.833 --> 00:00:06.399
chart

00:00:06.611 --> 00:00:07.401
measured on a

00:00:07.213 --> 00:00:08.604
fixed code corpus

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:09.736
micjm points to

00:00:09.548 --> 00:00:10.302
a paper

00:00:10.822 --> 00:00:11.930
The cited paper

00:00:11.742 --> 00:00:12.767
trains the same

00:00:12.579 --> 00:00:14.053
160M model on

00:00:13.864 --> 00:00:15.090
five data mixes

00:00:14.902 --> 00:00:16.034
that vary only

00:00:15.846 --> 00:00:16.517
the share of

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:16.883
code

00:00:17.414 --> 00:00:18.510
The author's point

00:00:18.381 --> 00:00:19.112
is not that

00:00:18.923 --> 00:00:19.642
the chart is

00:00:19.454 --> 00:00:20.220
meaningless

00:00:20.327 --> 00:00:20.998
but that it

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:21.859
needs far more

00:00:21.671 --> 00:00:22.697
qualification

00:00:22.744 --> 00:00:24.206
which OpenAI itself

00:00:24.018 --> 00:00:24.630
gives in a

00:00:24.442 --> 00:00:26.270
footnote about GPT-2

00:00:26.081 --> 00:00:27.543
and GPT-3

00:00:28.062 --> 00:00:29.076
Full story on

00:00:28.887 --> 00:00:29.783
the dashboard
